      "knowledge_point": "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions",
      "problem": "Express ${cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})$ as a single trigonometric ratio.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
